Varun & Ramana,

As a take away from the conversation yesterday and input for the discussion=
 to happen later today, which I believe is going to include error handling =
in addition to integrations between DLH and PAX, I have a summarization of =
errors that occurred in DLH processes over a 10 week sample that was captur=
ed from 8/25 back to 6/14.



Over 80% of the logged failures were in the "FTP to BLOB"  actions, which i=
s the retrieval of the raw files and saving to BLOB.

90% of the  "FTP to BLOB" failures were with retrieval of files from PBM Op=
tum for the numerous Optum clients. This is a high false positive due to Op=
tum not providing files on days when there are no eligible claims, but we c=
heck daily for all expected files. Days when a file is not found an error i=
s logged, this does not cause a support action for the DLH EDM team however=
 the business PAX Product owner does manually verify when no file was recei=
ved vs Optum does not have claims to report using a reporting portal with O=
ptum.

The remaining 10% of "FTP to BLOB" errors are legit due to FTP connections =
failing or timing out, files being late, etc.



13% of all failures logged were in "BLOB to ACQ RAW", meaning the source fi=
le was successfully retrieved and saved to BLOB but failed to insert to the=
 ACQ schema.

83% of the "BLOB to ACQ RAW" failures resulted from claims files sourced fr=
om BMICH as empty files, with no records to load to ACQ an exception is log=
ged. An enhancement to the exception handling to stop the empty files from =
logging as an exception is in backlog but is a low priority. The remaining =
17% of "BLOB to ACQ RAW" failures are real Execution or Bugs needing to be =
resolved by DLH support.



Actual support response efforts from DLH team:

Missing Files - FTP to BLOB
Other than Optum, we expect to receive files from all clients. If a file is=
 missing, DLH team reports it to the PAX product owner for follow up with t=
he file source. Due to the criticality of daily files being received and lo=
aded this is a combination of automated and manual support & communication.

Execution Failures - any of the ETL processes in DLH
These are usually caused by Azure execution issues. When this happens, the =
failures are typically resolved with a manual restart the pipeline.

Unexpected Failures
These are often caused by issues with source files or problems in ADF. If i=
t's a source file issue, DLH team coordinates with the account manager of t=
he client with the data issue. If it's a code issue, that can be resolved w=
ith a code change a backlog item is created and worked to resolve the root =
cause.

Manual Processes
Some processes are still manual, low frequency, low effort to manually exec=
ute with a backlog item to be automated.
Wrike Requests
Frequent requests from account managers for Client support and enhancements=
, new client implementations, as well as support for Analytics using the DL=
H.



Jeff















Jeff Trzebiatowski

